# Current GUI action and retained resume filename
Workstream-ID: lea-29
GitHub-Issue: https://github.com/Zanark/LinkedIn-EasyApply-Automaton/issues/29
Publication: PUBLIC_SAFE_PROJECTION
Source-record: Reviewed bounded native Run-page status improvement
Status: DONE
Milestone: See the current operation and last selected application resume
Dependencies: lea-20

## Purpose
Show an operation-specific icon above the current action name beside live run
counters. Show the last resume filename chosen for an application beside the
run controls, retaining it until another application resume is selected.

## Tasks
- [x] Trace native layout, process lifecycle and authoritative selection boundaries.
- [x] Preserve preceding work and establish an isolated fictional development lane.
- [x] Add bounded opt-in activity updates without weakening existing metrics.
- [x] Implement themed action and retained filename indicators with native help.
- [x] Qualify process streaming, engine boundaries, lifecycle and narrow layouts.
- [x] Update affected guides and reconcile only this workstream's tracking.

## Acceptance criteria
- [x] An icon above its action name identifies the actual current operation.
- [x] Stages, interactive waits and process completion/stop/error remain distinct.
- [x] The filename reflects the final static or tailored application choice.
- [x] Other jobs, missing choices, scans, tools and run completion do not clear it.
- [x] A later authoritative selection replaces it; long names remain inspectable.
- [x] Malformed/partial status records do not corrupt logs, prompts or counters.
- [x] Existing metrics clients and ordinary terminal output remain compatible.
- [x] Both themes and compact/expanded layouts work with two-line hover help.
- [x] Fictional qualification preserves private inputs and independent applications.
- [x] One issue, one Project item and a persistent backlink represent this workstream.

## Completion evidence
The source Run page now shows a themed operation icon above its action name
beside the counters. Actual browser, search, resume, form, upload, approval,
submission checking, outreach, invitation and result boundaries emit finite
activity updates. Command lifecycle and unavailable status remain distinct.

Separate version-two metadata requires both GUI capability flags; unchanged
version-one counters remain compatible. Invalid activity is reported without
disabling counters. Only the final static-or-tailored application choice emits
a basename. The native filename display retains it for this window's lifetime,
supports middle elision and full escaped hover text, and does not imply upload
or submission. Scans, tools, missing choices and finished runs never clear it.

An isolated public-source candidate passes all 449 coupled GUI, process,
protocol and workflow regressions. Black and Ruff pass all seventeen changed
Python files; mypy passes seventy-three implementation files. Four actual
fictional dark/light scenes at minimum and expanded sizes were reviewed.
Compact counters, action, command controls, logs and prompt input fit without
overlap; geometry assertions cover the actual minimum height and text sizes.

Exactly twenty qualified source, test and guide files were promoted and
byte-verified. Earlier additions in shared files, unaffected public payloads,
selected private metadata, unchanged HEAD and empty staging are preserved.
Only dashboard commands were observed before promotion; no independent
application was controlled. Affected GUI, tutorial and lifecycle guides
distinguish this source update from existing release screenshots and downloads.

This is bounded local technical completion. The issue/Project settlement is
independently recorded in the excluded index and development ledger.
No live application, human acceptance, restart, commit, push or release is claimed.

